{"symbol": "TreeWalk::absorb", "final_verdict": "confirmed", "rationale": "Traced and empirically reproduced. In `absorb`, the visited-check gates both the frontier push and (via `continue`) the `collected.push(entry)` for that same `Sent` entry. `visited` is populated only when a mailbox is dequeued via `next_mailbox`, so a `Sent` discovered later (in a different mailbox's ring) whose `recipient` was an earlier-visited mailbox — the ping-pong/reply case — is dropped from `collected` entirely, not merely skipped for re-enqueueing. Because `fold_nodes_with` requires a collected `Sent` entry to build a node (`let sent = node.sent?;`), that mail_id's node vanishes from the stitched tree even though its `Received`/`Finished` entries (unconditionally collected, since only `Sent` is guarded) are present.

I verified this is not hypothetical: added a temporary test (`chassis(0) -> observer(10) -> leaf_a(20)`, with leaf_a replying back to the already-visited `observer`) and ran it against the current code — it failed, with the reply's `mail_id` absent from `mails` (only root and child_a present). I then applied exactly the suggested fix (only guard the frontier push; always `collected.push(entry)`) and reran the full `trace::walk` test module: all 6 pre-existing tests still pass (including `guided_walk_dedups_diamond_recipient`, which never actually exercises the true branch of the visited-check because the diamond's shared recipient is discovered via frontier push, not yet in `visited`, at absorb time — so the fix changes no existing test's outcome) plus the new repro test now passes. I reverted the worktree to its original state afterward (`git checkout -- .../walk.rs`); no changes were left in place.

This meets the strict correctness bar: a concrete input (reply-to-visited-mailbox) and code path (the `continue` before `collected.push`) were named and the misbehavior was demonstrated by a runnable test, and the suggested fix is confirmed strictly better — it fixes real data loss and does not regress any currently-passing test."}

{"symbol": "TreeWalk::absorb", "final_verdict": "confirmed", "rationale": "Traced the exact control flow: in `absorb`, the `continue` inside `if self.visited.contains(&recipient) { continue; }` is inside the outer `for entry in entries` loop, not scoped only to the `if let TraceEvent::Sent` block — so it skips the rest of that loop iteration entirely, including the unconditional `self.collected.push(entry)` below it. That means any `Sent` event whose recipient mailbox has already been visited is dropped from `collected` outright, not merely excluded from being re-enqueued onto the frontier.

Concrete failure path: given the existing test topology in `guided_walk_reconstructs_tree_across_rings` (chassis -> observer -> {leaf_a, leaf_b}, leaf_a -> grandchild, BFS visit order chassis, observer, leaf_a, leaf_b, grandchild), suppose grandchild sends a reply back to leaf_a (a `Sent` recorded in grandchild's own ring, since `Sent` lands in the sender's ring per this module's model). By the time grandchild's ring is absorbed, leaf_a is already in `visited` (its ring was fetched earlier in BFS order). The reply's `Sent` entry is therefore skipped by `continue` and never reaches `collected`. In `fold_nodes_with`, a `MailNodeWire` requires `node.sent` (`let sent = node.sent?;`), so this reply mail_id — even if its `Received`/`Finished` had already landed in leaf_a's own ring (fetched earlier, but a post-settlement walk means leaf_a's ring already contains all its history at fetch time, so those aren't lost) — has no `sent` field and is filtered out of `mails` entirely by the `filter_map`. The reply node silently vanishes from the reconstructed tree, exactly as the finder describes: a ping-pong/reply hop back to an earlier-visited actor loses its node.

No existing test exercises this back-edge/reply scenario — all four tests in the file (`stitch_folds_events_per_mail_id_regardless_of_order`, `stitch_missing_root_sent_is_not_found`, `stitch_counts_unfinished_nodes_as_in_flight`, `guided_walk_reconstructs_tree_across_rings`, `guided_walk_dedups_diamond_recipient`) only cover forward-only trees or same-batch diamonds where the shared recipient is not yet visited when both entries arrive, so the visited-recipient collect-skip path is never hit. Applying the suggested fix (only guard the `frontier.push_back`, keep `collected.push(entry)` unconditional) would not change the outcome of any of these existing tests, since none of them rely on the drop behavior — it strictly adds correctness for the untested back-edge case without regressing anything. This is a straightforward, deterministic control-flow bug (not a subtle numeric/sign convention), verifiable directly from the source without needing to run the suite."}

{"symbol": "Atlas::pack (shelf_height accumulator)", "final_verdict": "confirmed", "rationale": "Traced pack()'s shelf-advance math: shelf_x advances by padded_width (line 210, includes GLYPH_PADDING), giving a real 1px horizontal gutter between glyphs on the same shelf. But shelf_height accumulates the unpadded height (line 211: `.max(height)`), and the next shelf's y-origin is `shelf_y + shelf_height` (line 202) — using that same unpadded value. So for the tallest glyph on a shelf, its bottom row sits at `y + height`, which is exactly where the next shelf starts: zero vertical gap, unlike the enforced horizontal gap. This contradicts the module's own stated invariant ("Side length... transparent-pixel gutter between packed glyphs so bilinear sampling... never bleeds"), and the render pipeline (`crates/aether-substrate/src/render/quad.rs:128-129`) confirms `FilterMode::Linear` sampling, so the bleed is a real rendering artifact, not a hypothetical.

